A component author chooses where representation matters. A slot can collect
text, a sequence can collect node descriptions and an ordinary function can
turn those descriptions into an HTML String for an entry.

### Reuse a group of inline styles

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    heading #= [$html:<h2 style="[$slot style]">[$slot]</h2>]
    label #= [$html:<span style="[$slot style]">[$slot]</span>]
    heavy #= [$in style:font-weight: 700;]
    red #= [$in style:color: red;]

    style_text #= [:[$slot style]]
    warning #= [$in style:[style_text, heavy, red]]

    [heading, warning:Breaking change]
    [label, warning:Deprecated]
]

`style_text` collects the named style inserts, then `warning` targets the
immediate element's `style` slot with the resulting declarations. A function
returning a String can also build CSS, but its caller has to name the target
slot. An insert cannot travel through a function result or search an ancestor.

### Collect typed children

Suppose your package defines `HtmlNode` plus `link`, `aside` and `render_html`.
The signature of `aside` takes a sequence of nodes and returns one node.
That parameter supplies the bracket construction's element type.

[codeblock, $code "moth":
    sidebar = aside([:
        [link("Home", "/")]
        [loop pages |page|:
            [link(page.title, page.url)]
        ]
    ])
    html = render_html(sidebar)
]

Each iteration contributes one link node. The ordinary `aside` call constructs
the parent and `render_html` supplies the String. A wrapper named `aside`
would instead receive only the slots it declared. The language and HTML builder
give these package-defined node types no special treatment.

### Keep resource contributions separate

The Basic page's `HtmlPart` carries one tracked stylesheet beside its body.
The entry explicitly forwards each resource to its own direct `stylesheets`
insert. The builder resolves its URL and deduplicates by stable origin. For
shared selectors or responsive rules, use a CSS file. An inline `$css`
declaration suits an instance-specific value.

The `head` destination deduplicates each complete rendered contribution.
Combining several head snippets into one String changes that boundary, so
keep independent snippets in separate direct inserts.

### Types and boundaries keep reuse safe

A nominal `HtmlPart` gives the library a checked contract. It never converts an
arbitrary record automatically. Const construction checks every field and every
called function. Reusable source packages publish only selected declarations
through `$export`.

Runtime components and browser handles still obey Moth's shared and exclusive
access rules and mandatory lifetime analysis. Returning a String from a const
function grants no retained DOM reference and no implicit subscription.
Explicit `Error!` contracts and ordinary recovery handle expected runtime
failures. A const function that the compiler cannot evaluate reports a
diagnostic instead of quietly becoming runtime work.

### When to use the pattern

Reach for a reusable `HtmlPart` result when a navbar, sidebar, tabbed docs
section or pager owns markup plus resource dependencies. A straightforward
article can stay a direct top-level `["[$md: ...]"]` fragment. Keep runtime
behaviour in a separately invoked function, covered in
@../browser-behaviour/ (Browser behaviour).

See @../../constants (Constants), @../../resources (Resources) and
@../../packages/core/html/ (@core/html).
